      "id": "net-security",
      "name": "Network Security",
      "scenarios": [
        {
          "id": "net-plus-fw-telnet",
          "title": "The Telnet rule",
          "situation": "A firewall audit finds Telnet open to the internet on a router's outside interface. Credentials cross that session in cleartext.",
          "prompt": "What is the correct firewall action?",
          "choices": [
            "Deny inbound TCP 23 from untrusted zones and require SSH (TCP 22) for remote administration",
            "Leave Telnet open but change the password monthly",
            "Block TCP 22 instead \u2014 SSH is the risky one",
            "Open Telnet wider so admins can connect faster"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Telnet (TCP 23) sends everything \u2014 including usernames and passwords \u2014 in cleartext, so it has no place on an untrusted interface. The fix is a firewall rule denying inbound 23 from outside, with SSH (TCP 22, encrypted) as the administrative path. A stronger password on a cleartext protocol protects nothing from a sniffer.",
          "hint": "The password is strong, but the wire is glass. What sees everything?",
          "objectives": [
            "Firewalls",
            "Hardening"
          ],
          "mins": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "net-plus-ids-ips",
          "title": "Detect or prevent?",
          "situation": "A security architect must choose between IDS and IPS for the data-center edge. The requirement: stop the attack in progress, not just log it.",
          "prompt": "Which fits, and what is the trade-off?",
          "choices": [
            "IPS \u2014 inline, it can drop malicious traffic; the trade-off is that a false positive can block legitimate traffic",
            "IDS \u2014 it prevents attacks by detecting them loudly",
            "Either \u2014 they are the same product with different names",
            "Neither \u2014 firewalls already do this"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "IDS is passive: it watches a copy of traffic and alerts. IPS sits inline and can actually drop or reset malicious sessions \u2014 which meets the 'stop it' requirement. The cost of inline prevention is risk: a false positive blocks real business traffic, so IPS tuning is an ongoing discipline, not a one-time install.",
          "hint": "One watches and shouts; the other stands in the road. Which one can take the hit?",
          "objectives": [
            "IDS vs IPS",
            "Security devices"
          ],
          "mins": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "net-plus-vpn-remote",
          "title": "The remote worker's tunnel",
          "situation": "An employee working from home needs secure access to internal file shares. The traffic crosses the public internet.",
          "prompt": "Which solution fits, and which VPN type is it?",
          "choices": [
            "A client-to-site (remote-access) VPN \u2014 encrypted tunnel from the laptop to the corporate network",
            "A site-to-site VPN between their home router and HQ",
            "Port-forward SMB (TCP 445) directly through the firewall",
            "Email the files back and forth instead"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Remote-access (client-to-site) VPNs exist for exactly this: one roaming user tunneling into the corporate network over encrypted IPsec or SSL/TLS. Site-to-site connects whole networks, not individuals. Exposing SMB to the internet is asking for ransomware, and email is not a file-access strategy.",
          "hint": "One laptop, hostile internet, private shares. What wraps the laptop's traffic in armor?",
          "objectives": [
            "VPNs",
            "Remote access"
          ],
          "mins": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "net-plus-rogue-ap",
          "title": "The evil twin",
          "situation": "Users report a new open network named 'CorpWiFi-Free' with a stronger signal than the corporate SSID. Several laptops have auto-joined it.",
          "prompt": "What is happening and what is the response?",
          "choices": [
            "Rogue/evil-twin AP conducting on-path attacks \u2014 contain it, find it physically, and enforce WPA3-Enterprise with certificate validation so clients reject impostors",
            "It is just a helpful neighbor boosting our signal \u2014 thank them",
            "Rename the corporate SSID to match it",
            "Turn off all Wi-Fi to be safe"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "An evil twin mimics the legitimate SSID to harvest credentials and intercept traffic \u2014 a classic on-path attack. Respond by locating the rogue device (wireless intrusion detection helps), removing it, and hardening clients: WPA3-Enterprise with proper server-certificate validation makes clients refuse the impostor instead of auto-joining it.",
          "hint": "Same name, stronger signal, no password. What is it fishing for?",
          "objectives": [
            "Wireless attacks",
            "Rogue AP"
          ],
          "mins": 7
        },
        {
          "id": "net-plus-arp-spoof",
          "title": "The poisoned cache",
          "situation": "Users' traffic to the gateway is intermittently redirected through an unknown MAC address. The gateway's IP now maps to two different MACs on different machines.",
          "prompt": "What attack is this, and what mitigates it at the switch?",
          "choices": [
            "ARP spoofing/poisoning \u2014 mitigate with Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI) validating ARP against the DHCP snooping binding table",
            "DNS spoofing \u2014 change the DNS servers",
            "A routing loop \u2014 enable STP",
            "Normal behavior \u2014 ARP always maps one IP to two MACs"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "ARP has no authentication, so an attacker can poison caches and claim to be the gateway \u2014 the textbook on-path (man-in-the-middle) setup. Dynamic ARP Inspection on the switch checks every ARP packet against the trusted DHCP-snooping binding table and drops the lies. One IP mapping to two MACs is never normal.",
          "hint": "ARP trusts everyone. What happens when someone lies \u2014 and who should check IDs at the door?",
          "objectives": [
            "Network attacks",
            "Switch security"
          ],
          "mins": 8
        },
        {
          "id": "net-plus-switch-harden",
          "title": "The open closet",
          "situation": "A wiring closet is accessible to anyone in the building. Unused switch ports are live, all ports are in the default VLAN, and the switch still uses factory credentials.",
          "prompt": "What is the hardening checklist?",
          "choices": [
            "Change default credentials, disable unused ports, assign ports to proper VLANs, enable port security \u2014 and lock the closet",
            "It is an internal network, so no hardening is needed",
            "Just change the credentials; the rest is overkill",
            "Unplug the switch when not in use"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Physical access plus live unused ports is an open invitation: anyone can plug in and join the default VLAN. Hardening is layered \u2014 credentials, disabled ports, VLAN assignment, port security (limiting MACs per port) \u2014 and the cheapest control of all is a locked door. 'Internal' is not a security boundary once the closet is public.",
          "hint": "The door is open and the ports are live. How many invitations is that?",
          "objectives": [
            "Hardening",
            "Access control"
          ],
          "mins": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "net-plus-segmentation",
          "title": "The flat network",
          "situation": "A malware outbreak on one workstation spreads to every server in minutes \u2014 the entire company is one flat broadcast domain with no internal filtering.",
          "prompt": "What architectural change contains the next outbreak?",
          "choices": [
            "Network segmentation \u2014 VLANs and firewall zones separating workstations, servers, and sensitive systems so a compromise cannot roam freely",
            "Faster antivirus on every machine",
            "A bigger internet pipe",
            "More frequent password changes"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Segmentation limits blast radius: when workstations, servers, and crown-jewel systems live in separate zones with firewall policy between them, malware that lands on a desktop cannot walk straight into the database. Endpoint tools help, but architecture is what turns one infected PC from a company-wide event into a contained incident.",
          "hint": "One infected PC, whole company down. What walls were missing?",
          "objectives": [
            "Segmentation",
            "Defense in depth"
          ],
          "mins": 7
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "net-troubleshoot",
      "name": "Network Troubleshooting",
      "scenarios": [
        {
          "id": "net-plus-method-ping",
          "title": "Where do you ping first?",
          "situation": "A user has no network access. You sit down at their PC with the troubleshooting methodology in mind.",
          "prompt": "What is the correct first-test order?",
          "choices": [
            "Ping loopback (127.0.0.1), then the default gateway, then a remote host \u2014 inside out, isolating each segment",
            "Ping a remote website first \u2014 if it works, everything is fine",
            "Reinstall the NIC driver first \u2014 drivers are usually the problem",
            "Replace the cable first \u2014 cables are usually the problem"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Inside-out testing isolates the fault's segment: loopback proves the local TCP/IP stack, the gateway proves the LAN path, a remote host proves routing and beyond. The first failure tells you exactly which segment to work in. Starting remote tells you only 'broken' \u2014 starting with parts replacement skips diagnosis entirely.",
          "hint": "If the loopback fails, do you need to check the cable? Let the order answer.",
          "objectives": [
            "Troubleshooting methodology",
            "Connectivity testing"
          ],
          "mins": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "net-plus-traceroute",
          "title": "Where do the packets die?",
          "situation": "A branch office cannot reach a cloud application. Ping to the app fails; ping to the local gateway works.",
          "prompt": "Which tool pinpoints the failing hop?",
          "choices": [
            "traceroute (tracert on Windows) \u2014 it lists each hop so you can see exactly where responses stop",
            "ipconfig \u2014 it shows the failing hop",
            "nslookup \u2014 it traces the route",
            "netstat \u2014 it lists the hops"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Traceroute sends packets with increasing TTL values, forcing each router along the path to reveal itself \u2014 the last responding hop before the timeouts is where the path breaks. Ping only says 'unreachable'; traceroute says 'unreachable, and here is precisely where.' That turns a vague WAN ticket into a specific provider or device to engage.",
          "hint": "Ping says no. Who says where?",
          "objectives": [
            "Troubleshooting tools",
            "traceroute"
          ],
          "mins": 5
        },
        {
          "id": "net-plus-dns-tool",
          "title": "The name that will not resolve",
          "situation": "Users can reach the new intranet server by IP but not by its hostname. You suspect DNS.",
          "prompt": "Which tool queries DNS directly, and what do you check?",
          "choices": [
            "nslookup (or dig) \u2014 query the hostname and confirm which server answers and what it returns",
            "ping \u2014 it is the DNS testing tool",
            "tracert \u2014 it resolves names hop by hop",
            "arp -a \u2014 it shows DNS entries"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "nslookup and dig speak DNS directly: they show which server answered and what record it returned, separating 'the record is wrong/missing' from 'the client is asking the wrong server.' Ping uses the resolver but does not interrogate it \u2014 when DNS is the suspect, question DNS itself.",
          "hint": "The suspect is the phone book. Which tool reads the phone book directly?",
          "objectives": [
            "DNS troubleshooting",
            "nslookup/dig"
          ],
          "mins": 5
        },
        {
          "id": "net-plus-cable-move",
          "title": "The link that died in the move",
          "situation": "A workstation worked Friday, was moved to a new desk Monday, and now shows 'network cable unplugged'. The cable clicks into both ends.",
          "prompt": "What is the systematic check?",
          "choices": [
            "Verify link lights, try a known-good cable, confirm T568B on both ends with a cable tester, and try a different switch port",
            "Reinstall Windows \u2014 moves corrupt the OS",
            "Replace the workstation \u2014 moves kill PCs",
            "Call the ISP \u2014 the internet is down"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "A move changes exactly one thing: the physical path. Link lights give instant feedback, a known-good cable eliminates the most abused component, a tester verifies the pairs survived the move, and a different port rules out a dead jack. The methodology says test the changed thing first \u2014 everything else is untouched.",
          "hint": "What is the only thing that changed between Friday and Monday?",
          "objectives": [
            "Cable troubleshooting",
            "Physical layer"
          ],
          "mins": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "net-plus-duplex",
          "title": "The slow, collision-filled link",
          "situation": "A link negotiates at 100 Mbps but throughput is terrible; the switch shows rising collision and FCS error counters on that port.",
          "prompt": "What is the likely cause?",
          "choices": [
            "Duplex mismatch \u2014 one side half-duplex, the other full-duplex",
            "The cable is too short",
            "Too much bandwidth is available",
            "The switch needs a firmware upgrade"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "In a duplex mismatch, the half-duplex side treats the full-duplex side's transmissions as collisions and backs off, while the full side never backs off \u2014 the result is exactly this: link up, throughput destroyed, collision and FCS counters climbing. The fix is matching duplex settings, normally by leaving both sides on auto-negotiation.",
          "hint": "One side politely waits its turn; the other talks over it. What breaks?",
          "objectives": [
            "Duplex",
            "Interface errors"
          ],
          "mins": 7
        },
        {
          "id": "net-plus-port-blocked",
          "title": "Works here, not there",
          "situation": "An application works for users on the LAN but fails for users across the WAN. The server is up and the app listens on TCP 8443.",
          "prompt": "What is the most likely cause and the check?",
          "choices": [
            "A firewall in the path is blocking TCP 8443 \u2014 verify with a port test and check the listening socket with ss/netstat on the server",
            "The application is broken for everyone",
            "The WAN is down",
            "Users need new PCs"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "'Works locally, fails remotely' is the signature of a path filter: something between the WAN users and the server is dropping TCP 8443. Confirm the server is actually listening (ss -tuln), then test the port from the remote side and walk the firewall rules in between. The app is proven working \u2014 the path is the suspect.",
          "hint": "Same app, same server, different network \u2014 different result. What sits between them?",
          "objectives": [
            "Firewall troubleshooting",
            "Port testing"
          ],
          "mins": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "net-plus-wireshark",
          "title": "The intermittent application",
          "situation": "An application fails randomly \u2014 no pattern by time, user, or location. Logs show nothing conclusive.",
          "prompt": "What is the deepest diagnostic step when all else is inconclusive?",
          "choices": [
            "Capture packets with Wireshark at both ends and compare \u2014 the wire does not lie about retransmits, resets, or malformed traffic",
            "Reboot everything weekly as preventive medicine",
            "Blame the ISP without evidence",
            "Rewrite the application"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "When symptoms are intermittent and logs are silent, a packet capture is ground truth: you can see the actual TCP handshakes, retransmissions, resets, and timing that no log recorded. Capturing at both ends distinguishes 'never sent' from 'sent but lost.' It is the deepest tool because it observes reality instead of inferring it.",
          "hint": "Everyone's logs say nothing. What sees everything that actually crossed the wire?",
          "objectives": [
            "Packet analysis",
            "Wireshark"
          ],
          "mins": 7
        }
      ]
    },
